            <ArticleTitle>Chronic thoracolumbar subdural empyema: Case report and surgical management.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>167</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/sni.sni_171_17</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Spinal cord abscesses and spinal subdural empyemas are rare and difficult to treat.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE DESCRIPTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 35-year-old male presented to an outside institution with 2 months of progressive low back pain, weakness, and bowel incontinence; he was diagnosed with an L4 epidural abscess that was poorly managed. When the patient presented to our institution, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) revealed a well-organized chronic subdural abscess at the thoracolumbar junction. Following resection, his back pain resolved but he was left with a residual paraparesis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Subdural abscesses are rare and should be considered among the differential diagnoses for intraspinal mass lesions. Treatment should include prompt surgical exploration and decompression combined with appropriate prolonged antibiotic treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
